\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces System architecture: the Extract, Model, and Evaluate layers. The development data (MIMIC-IV) flows through cohort construction, hourly panel assembly, and onset labelling into the seven statistical analyses; the frozen model coefficients are then transported to eICU-CRD for external validation.}}{17}{figure.4.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Boxplots of heart rate, lactate, and respiratory rate by sepsis status. All three features show genuine separation, but no single feature cleanly discriminates the groups.}}{18}{figure.4.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Kernel density estimate of maximum lactate by sepsis status. Both groups are right-skewed; the septic distribution is shifted upward but overlaps heavily with the non-septic distribution.}}{18}{figure.4.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Distribution of sepsis-onset hour among septic stays. The spike at hour 0 marks prevalent sepsis; the right tail is incident sepsis developing in the ICU, which is the target for early prediction. The dashed line marks the hour-six landmark.}}{19}{figure.4.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Nomogram for incident Sepsis-3 onset within 12 hours at the hour-six landmark. Each predictor contributes points on the top scale; the total is mapped to a predicted risk on the bottom scale. The ``was-measured'' indicators (\_msg features) contribute substantial points, reflecting the informative-missingness signal.}}{21}{figure.4.5}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Cumulative incidence of sepsis onset from the hour-six landmark, stratified by SIRS status. Patients with $\ge 2$ SIRS criteria develop sepsis faster, confirming that inflammatory markers carry predictive signal for the incident-onset task.}}{25}{figure.5.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Cumulative incidence functions from the hour-six landmark accounting for death without sepsis as a competing risk, stratified by SIRS status. The solid lines show onset incidence; the dashed lines show the competing risk of death without sepsis.}}{26}{figure.5.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Left: mean hourly heart rate by sepsis status; septic patients run higher and diverge over time. Right: distribution of within-patient lag-1 autocorrelation of heart rate; the mean of 0.57 confirms strong hour-to-hour dependence that the static analysis discarded.}}{27}{figure.5.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Calibration curve (out-of-sample) for the Analysis-1 elastic-net nomogram at the hour-six landmark. Points near the diagonal indicate that predicted risks are usable as probabilities, not merely as a ranking.}}{30}{figure.5.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Dynamic AUC($s$): the supermodel's out-of-sample discrimination as a function of the hour at which the prediction is made, versus separate per-landmark models. The supermodel matches or exceeds the bespoke models at most landmarks while requiring only one set of coefficients.}}{31}{figure.5.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Time-dependent AUC for predicting sepsis onset over increasing horizons from the hour-six landmark. Discrimination remains above 0.70 across all tested horizons, demonstrating robust risk stratification over time.}}{33}{figure.5.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Alarm-burden trade-off for the real-time supermodel. High sensitivity ($\ge 90\%$) is achievable only at alarm rates (6.5 false alarms per patient-day) that would fatigue bedside staff. A practical operating point (51\% sensitivity, 0.7 false alarms per patient-day) balances detection against alarm fatigue.}}{35}{figure.5.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces GBTM: three latent SOFA-trajectory classes over the first 24 ICU hours. Class 1 (mildest) is flat near zero; Class 2 (intermediate) rises to SOFA $\sim $3; Class 3 (most severe) rises steeply to SOFA $\sim $7. The trajectory \emph {shape}, not just its endpoint, carries prognostic and predictive information.}}{37}{figure.5.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Love plot: covariate balance before (red) and after (teal) propensity-score matching. All standardised mean differences fall below 0.1 after matching, confirming adequate balance.}}{39}{figure.5.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces eICU calibration by risk decile: the frozen MIMIC model (red) over-predicts, and a single-parameter recalibration (blue) restores agreement with observed onset rates. The dashed grey line marks perfect calibration.}}{41}{figure.5.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces GAM spline: the non-linear relationship between lactate and log-odds of sepsis onset. The effect is monotone-increasing but saturates at high lactate values, supporting the log-transformation used in the linear models.}}{42}{figure.5.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces Left: precision-recall curve for the Analysis-1 nomogram (PR-AUC shown). Right: decision-curve analysis demonstrating net benefit above both ``treat all'' and ``treat none'' across threshold probabilities from 0.02 to 0.20.}}{42}{figure.5.12}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
